cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: >-
  assuregraph: an assurance case as a machine-checkable evidence graph
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-08
license: Apache-2.0
abstract: >-
  A declarative YAML format for an assurance case in Goal Structuring Notation
  (Core GSN), with six computed checks over the resulting directed graph:
  claims with no SupportedBy child, cited evidence artifacts absent from disk,
  evidence staleness detected by comparing an artifact's SHA-256 digest with
  the digest recorded when the claim cited it, assumptions not marked
  discharged, cyclic regions of the argument, and nodes unreachable from the
  declared top goals. Every finding names the offending node identifiers and
  the command-line interface exits nonzero when the case is structurally
  incomplete, so the checks can gate a build. The package also renders a case
  as a Mermaid flowchart with GSN shapes, and prints a coverage summary in
  which every fraction carries its denominator and an undefined fraction is
  reported as undefined rather than as zero or one hundred per cent. There is
  deliberately no learned component: whether an argument is sound is the
  judgement this tool refuses to automate. The content-hash staleness check
  detects that an artifact's bytes changed and not whether the change matters;
  the measured discrimination between meaning-inverting and meaning-preserving
  edits is zero, and this is published rather than implied away. Structural
  completeness says nothing about whether the evidence is adequate. A
  well-formed assurance case is not a safe system. This is not a DO-178C or
  ARP4754A compliance tool and supports no certification argument. Validation
  level 2, research grade. Not flight-qualified, not certified, not approved
  for operational aerospace use.
authors:
  - name: "OPTIMA Organisation"
keywords:
  - assurance-case
  - goal-structuring-notation
  - gsn
  - evidence-graph
  - content-hash
  - staleness
  - traceability
  - mermaid
  - aerospace-assurance
references:
  - type: standard
    title: "Goal Structuring Notation Community Standard, Version 3"
    number: "SCSC-141C"
    authors:
      - name: "SCSC Assurance Case Working Group"
    institution:
      name: "Safety-Critical Systems Club"
    year: 2021
    month: 5
    notes: >-
      The notation assuregraph transcribes: the six Core GSN element types, the
      two relationship types, the identifier prefixes used in the standard's
      examples, and the Undeveloped decorator. The version, document number,
      publisher and May 2021 date were read from scsc.uk on 2026-10-08. No
      clause or page number is quoted because none was verified in this
      environment. The standard's own document history records Version 1 issued
      16 November 2011 and Version 2 in January 2018.
  - type: standard
    title: "Secure Hash Standard (SHS)"
    number: "FIPS PUB 180-4"
    institution:
      name: "National Institute of Standards and Technology"
    year: 2015
    month: 8
    notes: >-
      SHA-256, the hash used for the evidence freshness check. The check detects
      that an artifact's bytes changed; it carries no information about whether
      the change matters.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Tarjan
        given-names: "R. E."
    title: "Depth-first search and linear graph algorithms"
    journal: "SIAM Journal on Computing"
    volume: 1
    issue: 2
    year: 1972
    notes: >-
      The strongly connected component algorithm behind the cycle check,
      implemented iteratively in assuregraph.graph. No page number is quoted
      because none was verified in this environment.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Johnson
        given-names: "D. B."
    title: "Finding all the elementary circuits of a directed graph"
    journal: "SIAM Journal on Computing"
    volume: 4
    issue: 1
    year: 1975
    notes: >-
      Cited for why assuregraph reports one witness cycle per cyclic region
      rather than enumerating every elementary cycle. No page number is quoted
      because none was verified in this environment.
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Denney
        given-names: "Ewen"
      - family-names: Pai
        given-names: "Ganesh"
    title: "Tool support for assurance case development"
    journal: "Automated Software Engineering"
    doi: "10.1007/s10515-017-0230-5"
    notes: >-
      AdvoCATE, the NASA Ames assurance case automation toolset named in the
      README's alternatives table. Volume, issue, year and page numbers are not
      quoted because they were not verified in this environment; the authors,
      title, journal and DOI were read from a NASA-hosted copy of the manuscript
      on 2026-10-08.
